1.19 INTRODUCTION TO C
======================================================================

C is a general-purpose programming language.

C was developed in the early 1970s at Bell Labs,
primarily by Dennis Ritchie.

C became highly influential because it provides:

    • Structured programming
    • Efficient execution
    • Portability
    • Direct memory manipulation through pointers
    • Low-level hardware access capabilities
    • A powerful standard library
    • Small core language


======================================================================
1.20 HISTORY OF C
======================================================================

Important historical development:

    BCPL
      ↓
      B
      ↓
      C


BCPL:

    Developed by Martin Richards.


B:

    Developed at Bell Labs by Ken Thompson,
    based on earlier systems-language work.


C:

    Developed by Dennis Ritchie at Bell Labs
    in the early 1970s.


C was heavily used in the development and rewriting
of the UNIX operating system.


Important people:

    Ken Thompson
        → Major contributor to UNIX and B

    Dennis Ritchie
        → Creator of C and major UNIX contributor


======================================================================
1.21 WHY WAS C CREATED?
======================================================================

C was designed to provide a good balance between:

    High-level programming features
                 +
    Low-level hardware control
                 +
    Efficient execution


This made C particularly useful for:

    • Operating systems
    • System software
    • Compilers
    • Embedded systems
    • Hardware-oriented software


======================================================================
1.22 FEATURES OF C
======================================================================

1. SIMPLE

    C has a relatively small core language.

2. PROCEDURAL

    Programs can be organized as functions and procedures
    that operate on data.

3. STRUCTURED

    Supports:

        • if
        • switch
        • loops
        • functions
        • blocks

4. PORTABLE

    Well-written C programs can often be moved between
    different platforms with limited changes.

5. EFFICIENT

    C allows programmers to write programs with relatively
    low runtime overhead.

6. LOW-LEVEL ACCESS

    Pointers and bit operations allow close interaction
    with memory and hardware.

7. MODULAR

    Programs can be divided into functions and source files.

8. EXTENSIBLE

    Programs can use libraries and programmer-defined
    functions.

9. FAST

    Compiled C programs can be highly efficient.

10. MANUAL MEMORY MANAGEMENT

    Dynamic memory can be managed using:

        malloc()
        calloc()
        realloc()
        free()


