======================================================================
1.26 #include <stdio.h>
======================================================================

Code:

    #include <stdio.h>


#include is a preprocessor directive.

It tells the preprocessor to include declarations from
the specified header file.

stdio.h means:

    Standard Input/Output Header


It contains declarations for functions such as:

    printf()
    scanf()
    getchar()
    putchar()
    fgets()
    puts()


Therefore, when we use:

    printf()

we normally include:

    #include <stdio.h>


IMPORTANT:

    #include is processed before the actual compilation
    of the C source file.


======================================================================
1.27 int main(void)
======================================================================

Code:

    int main(void)


main() is the function where program execution begins
in a hosted C environment.

    int
        → return type

    main
        → function name

    (void)
        → explicitly indicates that main takes no arguments


Why int?

    main returns an integer status to the operating system.

Example:

    return 0;


Conventionally means:

    Successful termination.


======================================================================
1.28 { AND }
======================================================================

Code:

    {
        ...
    }


Curly braces define a block.

Example:

    int main(void)
    {
        printf("Hello");
        return 0;
