1.36 PREPROCESSING
======================================================================

The preprocessor handles directives such as:

    #include
    #define
    #if
    #ifdef
    #ifndef


Example:

    #include <stdio.h>


The preprocessor processes the inclusion directive
before compilation.

Output is conceptually an expanded source file.


======================================================================
1.37 COMPILATION
======================================================================

The compiler analyzes C source code and translates it
into a lower-level representation.

It performs tasks such as:

    • Syntax checking
    • Semantic analysis
    • Type checking
    • Code generation/translation


If the program contains an invalid C statement,
the compiler may report an error.


Example:

    int main(void)
    {
        int x = ;
        return 0;
    }


This is invalid C and should produce a diagnostic.


======================================================================
1.38 ASSEMBLY
======================================================================

The compiler may produce assembly code or an equivalent
lower-level representation.

The assembler then converts assembly instructions
into object code.

Conceptually:

    Assembly
       ↓
    Assembler
       ↓
    Object file


======================================================================
1.39 LINKING
======================================================================

A C program often uses functions from libraries.

Example:

    printf()


The compiler processes the call, while the linker resolves
references between object files and libraries.

Conceptually:

    Your object file
          +
    Library/object files
          ↓
       Linker
          ↓
      Executable


This is why linker errors are different from compiler errors.


======================================================================
1.40 LOADING
======================================================================

After compilation and linking:

    Executable
        ↓
    Operating System


The operating system loads the program into memory
and prepares it for execution.

The CPU then begins executing the program according
to the platform's execution model.


